  "term": "brass",
  "says": "Reid's brass: a saw (the lip buzz, all harmonics present) through a low-pass whose cutoff is driven by an envelope that overshoots - it opens quickly past where it will settle, then falls back to a sustain level, the 'blip' that makes a brass note speak - while the amplitude rises over a few tens of milliseconds rather than at once; moderate resonance, a little detune between two saws for section width, no velocity to brightness beyond the envelope. Signature: an attack between 30 and 200 ms, harmonic, a centroid in the low mids once settled, the brightness peaking just after the onset then dropping by a few semitones as the filter envelope settles.",
